\label{Matrix.isPosSemidef_and_invertible_iff_isPosDef}
  If $A\in{M_n}$. Then\\
  \hspace*{0.5cm}$0\leq{A}$ and is invertible $\,\Leftrightarrow\,$ $A$ is positive-definite.

{\ }
 \begin{description}
  \item[$(\Rightarrow)$]
   Suppose $0\leq{A}$ and $A=A^{1/2}A^{1/2}$ is invertible. Then $A^{1/2}$ is also invertible and positive semi-definite. Let $v\in\mathbb{C}^n$ be non-zero.
   Then, we compute,
   \[\ip{v}{Av}=\ip{v}{A^{1/2}A^{1/2}v}=\ip{A^{1/2}v}{A^{1/2}v}>0,\]
   since $A^{1/2}v\neq0$ (as $A^{1/2}$ is invertible). Note that, in the second equality, we use the self-adjointed-ness of $A^{1/2}$ since it is positive semi-definite. So we are done.
  \item[$(\Leftarrow)$]
   Suppose $0<A$. Then obviously $0\leq{A}$, so we only need to check if it is invertible. Suppose the contrary, i.e., $A$ is not invertible. Then there exists a non-zero $v\in\mathbb{C}^n$ such that $Av=0$. But then, by the hypothesis, we get $0<\ip{v}{Av}=0$, which is a contradiction. Thus $A$ is invertible.
 \end{description}

